#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""run-gitleaks - the G2 secrets-scan driver across the planes (P0.3.1).

Wraps the pinned, vendored, checksum-verified gitleaks BINARY (.gate-tools/bin/gitleaks) so the
range-base fallback chain is ONE testable place, not duplicated/forgotten across the hooks + CI:

  --staged   L1 (pre-commit):  `gitleaks git --staged .`  — the diff being committed.
  --range    L2 (pre-push):    `gitleaks git --log-opts=<base>..HEAD .` — all unpushed commits, the
             LAST local catch before a secret goes public. Base = the first of `@{u}` ->
             `origin/<branch>` -> `origin/main` -> `origin/HEAD` that resolves; if NONE resolve (first
             push to an empty remote) it FULL-scans every commit (no range), the safe direction. NOT
             skipped by the docs-only fastpath — a secret can live in a `.md`.
  --dir      L4 (CI clean-checkout): `gitleaks dir <repo root>` — the whole working tree.
  --history  L5 (nightly schedule):  `gitleaks git .` over the ENTIRE commit history (the CI job must
             check out with fetch-depth: 0; a default shallow checkout scans only the tip).

The positional argument of `gitleaks git` is the REPOSITORY PATH (`gitleaks git [flags] [repo]`),
so a commit range rides `--log-opts` and every `git` mode names the repository explicitly (git_scan).
The P0.3.1 range leg passed `<base>..HEAD` as that positional: gitleaks tried to chdir into it,
scanned 0 commits and exited 0, so the pre-push leg read nothing on every push until this fix.

All modes use the committed .gitleaks.toml + (if present) the .gitleaks-baseline.json and run --redact,
without -v and without a report file, so no finding's detail is printed (a custom rule reports a fixed
capture, not the value, so --redact alone would not mask the value). gitleaks LOGS a failure of its own
`git` call at level ERR and still exits 0, so its exit code alone cannot tell a clean scan from one that
read nothing: run_scan() relays the log and fails closed (exit 2) on any error-level line.

The committed allowlist + the capped baseline, both frozen by check-gitleaks-allowlist, are the only
suppression this repository controls. gitleaks' in-repo suppression channels are closed in every mode
(measured on the pinned 8.30.1):
  * an inline `gitleaks:allow` comment on the secret's line: `--ignore-gitleaks-allow`;
  * a `.gitleaksignore` fingerprint list: `--gitleaks-ignore-path` names a path that cannot exist
    (NO_IGNORE_PATH, so the working directory's file is not read), and the driver refuses to scan
    beside `<scan source>/.gitleaksignore`, which gitleaks reads whatever that flag says;
  * git's binary verdict: git writes "Binary files differ" in place of the diff, and gitleaks skips
    the file unread, for a `.gitattributes` `binary`/`-diff` mark (any depth) and for a file git's
    content check calls binary (one NUL byte). The `git` modes run with GIT_ATTR_SOURCE naming the
    empty tree (no in-tree attributes file is read) and a core.attributesFile holding `* diff` (every
    path is text), so git writes every file's diff. That needs git >= 2.41; an older git fails the
    scan closed.
One suppression stays, named: the global allowlist of gitleaks' bundled rule set, which
`[extend].useDefault` appends to the config. 8.30.1 has no switch that keeps the bundled rules and leaves
it out (its config.extend() appends it; no `[extend]` key and no flag drops it). It drops a finding of
every rule, a custom rule's too, by path - images, fonts, documents and binaries, Go module files,
vendored and dependency trees (vendor/, node_modules/, bower_components/, Python and Ruby package
trees), the Gradle and Maven wrappers, JS and Python lock files (pnpm-lock.yaml among them), minified
vendor libraries and any path containing `gitleaks.toml`. It also drops a finding by its secret: one
containing `false` in any case or either of two stopwords (the lowercase alphabet, one UUID), one
starting with `true` or ending in `null` (its first value regex, `(?i)^true|false|null$`, is an
unanchored alternation), or a whole placeholder shape (`$VAR`, `${VAR}`, `${{ secrets.X }}`, `{{ x }}`,
`%VAR%`, `@VAR@`, `%s`, `{0}`, one repeated letter, a /Users/... or /usr/...-style path). That value side
reaches the bundled rules only: each custom rule reports a fixed capture (.gitleaks.toml says why),
which none of those matches, so a MINISIGN_PASSWORD holding `False` reds. Accepted: G2 guards against
a careless leak of a text secret, those paths hold generated, binary or vendored artefacts, a bundled
rule's secret is a random token that holds such a word only by chance (a random 36-character GitHub
token holds `false` or ends in `null` with p ~ 2e-6), and a secret placed on purpose inside the
owner-acked tree is the G1 review's and the owner's surface. The pinned binary fixes both lists, and
the self-test pins their reach from both sides (the planted key reds in plain text paths and passes in
paths the list names; every planted secret with `False`, `null` or a stopword in its value reds; a
GitHub token the bundled rule reports passes with `false` spliced in), so a gitleaks bump that moves
either reds a leg.
The G24 self-test (g24-run-gitleaks.py) drives resolve_base(), the error-line classifier, run_scan()'s
verdict over a stand-in scanner, every mode end-to-end against a planted secret, every channel above per
mode beside the argv or environment that honoured it, and the reach of the bundled allowlist by path
and by value.
stdlib-only. L(-1) security-critical (scripts/l-neg1-files.toml, G71).

Usage: run-gitleaks {--staged | --range | --dir | --history}
Exit:  0 = clean; 1 = leak found / gitleaks error exit; 2 = bad invocation / gitleaks binary missing /
       gitleaks logged an error (a scan that errored is never reported clean) / a `.gitleaksignore`
       beside the scan source / a git older than 2.41.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path
for _stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):          # the console's codepage is not this script's concern (G9 invariant i)
    if hasattr(_stream, "reconfigure"):
        _stream.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
CONFIG = ROOT / ".gitleaks.toml"
BASELINE = ROOT / ".gitleaks-baseline.json"
IGNORE_FILE = ".gitleaksignore"
# [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P0.3.1] the --gitleaks-ignore-path value is a child of the config FILE, which main()
# requires to be a regular file, so no OS can hold anything there and gitleaks reads no ignore file through the
# flag; the null device would be read as an empty file instead (same verdict, through a per-OS Go special case).
NO_IGNORE_PATH = CONFIG / "no-gitleaksignore"
# [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P0.3.1] the `git` modes diff every file as text rather than refusing a `binary`/`-diff`
# mark or a NUL byte: a mark is legitimate on a real binary (the root .gitattributes marks the icons and the fuzz
# seeds), and git's content check calls any file with one NUL byte binary, so no tracked file and no byte decides what
# the scan reads - no in-tree attributes file (GIT_ATTR_SOURCE = the empty tree) and a core.attributesFile holding
# `* diff`, through the environment (no write to the object database). A git that ignores GIT_ATTR_SOURCE fails the
# scan closed - absent from git v2.40.0's environment.h and git(1), present in v2.41.0.
GIT_ATTR_SOURCE_MIN = (2, 41)
TEXT_ATTRIBUTES = "* diff\n"


def out(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"[G2] {msg}")


def fail(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"[G2] FAIL: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


def gitleaks_bin() -> str | None:
    for cand in (ROOT / ".gate-tools" / "bin" / "gitleaks.exe", ROOT / ".gate-tools" / "bin" / "gitleaks"):
        if cand.is_file():
            return str(cand)
    import shutil
    return shutil.which("gitleaks")


def git(*args: str) -> tuple[int, str]:
    p = subprocess.run(["git", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    return p.returncode, p.stdout.strip()


def _resolves(ref: str) -> bool:
    return git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", ref)[0] == 0


def resolve_base() -> str | None:
    """The L2 range base per the fallback chain; None => first push (full-scan)."""
    cands = ["@{u}"]
    rc, b = git("rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD")
    if rc == 0 and b and b != "HEAD":
        cands.append(f"origin/{b}")
    cands += ["origin/main", "origin/HEAD"]
    return next((c for c in cands if _resolves(c)), None)


# [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P0.3.1] one scan with the committed config, the bundled allowlist kept as the named
# residual: 8.30.1 cannot keep the bundled rules without it, and a second custom-rules-only pass would be a second
# scanner mode for paths that hold generated, binary or vendored artefacts; its value side reaches no custom rule (each
# reports a fixed capture) (the docstring names the reach, G24 legs pin it)
def common_args() -> list[str]:
    """The flags of every mode: the committed config, redaction, the leak exit code, no inline `gitleaks:allow`
    and no ignore file through --gitleaks-ignore-path; the baseline (capped by check-gitleaks-allowlist) when present."""
    args = ["--config", str(CONFIG), "--redact", "--no-banner", "--exit-code", "1",
            "--ignore-gitleaks-allow", "--gitleaks-ignore-path", str(NO_IGNORE_PATH)]
    if BASELINE.is_file():
        args += ["--baseline-path", str(BASELINE)]
    return args


def ignore_file_beside(source: Path) -> Path | None:
    """The `.gitleaksignore` gitleaks would read beside the scan source, or None. gitleaks 8.30.1 reads the flag's
    path, `<flag path>/.gitleaksignore` AND `<source>/.gitleaksignore` (three independent reads), so no flag keeps
    it from the last one: a fingerprint listed there drops the finding, and the driver never scans beside one.
    Any entry by that name counts (lexists: a file, a directory, a dangling link)."""
    path = source / IGNORE_FILE
    return path if os.path.lexists(path) else None


def attr_source_supported(version_text: str) -> bool:
    """(pure) Whether a `git version` line names a git that reads GIT_ATTR_SOURCE; an unparseable one does not."""
    mt = re.match(r"git version (\d+)\.(\d+)", version_text.strip())
    return mt is not None and (int(mt.group(1)), int(mt.group(2))) >= GIT_ATTR_SOURCE_MIN


def with_config_entry(env: dict[str, str], key: str, value: str) -> tuple[dict[str, str] | None, str]:
    """(pure) `env` with one GIT_CONFIG_* entry appended after the entries it already carries (git applies them in
    order, so the last value of a key wins), or (None, reason) when its GIT_CONFIG_COUNT is not a count."""
    raw = env.get("GIT_CONFIG_COUNT", "")
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]*", raw):
        return None, f"GIT_CONFIG_COUNT={raw!r} in the environment is not a count - the scan cannot add its git setting"
    n = int(raw or "0")
    return {**env, f"GIT_CONFIG_KEY_{n}": key, f"GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_{n}": value, "GIT_CONFIG_COUNT": str(n + 1)}, ""


def attribute_blind_env(scratch: Path) -> tuple[dict[str, str] | None, str]:
    """(env, "") for a `gitleaks git` scan - this environment with GIT_ATTR_SOURCE naming the empty tree and one more
    GIT_CONFIG_* entry naming `<scratch>/attributes` (`* diff`) as core.attributesFile, so the git that gitleaks
    spawns reads no in-tree .gitattributes and diffs every file as text: neither a `binary`/`-diff` mark nor a NUL
    byte hides a file's content - or (None, reason) when this git cannot do that."""
    rc, version = git("version")
    if rc != 0 or not attr_source_supported(version):
        return None, (f"`{version or 'git version'}` does not read GIT_ATTR_SOURCE (git >= "
                      f"{GIT_ATTR_SOURCE_MIN[0]}.{GIT_ATTR_SOURCE_MIN[1]}): a .gitattributes `binary`/`-diff` mark "
                      "would hide a file's content from the scan - upgrade git")
    p = subprocess.run(["git", "hash-object", "-t", "tree", "--stdin"], input="", capture_output=True, text=True,
                       encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    tree = p.stdout.strip()
    if p.returncode != 0 or not tree:
        return None, f"could not name the empty tree for GIT_ATTR_SOURCE: {p.stderr.strip()}"
    attributes = scratch / "attributes"
    attributes.write_bytes(TEXT_ATTRIBUTES.encode("ascii"))
    env, why = with_config_entry(dict(os.environ), "core.attributesFile", attributes.as_posix())
    if env is None:
        return None, why
    return {**env, "GIT_ATTR_SOURCE": tree}, ""


def git_scan(gl: str, *flags: str) -> list[str]:
    """The `gitleaks git` argv over the repository in the working directory. The repository is the
    command's only positional and is always passed explicitly as `.`; a commit range goes in `--log-opts`."""
    return [gl, "git", *flags, *common_args(), "."]


# a gitleaks console log line is `<time> <LEVEL> <message>` (zerolog), colour-coded even into a pipe
# [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P0.3.1] every error level counts (ERR, FTL, PNC), never an allow-list of
# known git messages: gitleaks itself logs git's rename-detection-limit notice at WRN and a git failure at
# ERR (both measured on the pinned 8.30.1), so its own level is the classification this gate reuses.
_ANSI_RE = re.compile(r"\x1b\[[0-9;]*m")
_ERROR_LEVELS = frozenset({"ERR", "FTL", "PNC"})


def error_lines(log: str) -> list[str]:
    """(pure) The gitleaks log lines at an error level, colour codes stripped. The level is read from the
    first two tokens only (`<time> <LEVEL> …`), so a message that merely contains `ERR` is not a hit."""
    hits = []
    for raw in log.splitlines():
        line = _ANSI_RE.sub("", raw).strip()
        if any(tok in _ERROR_LEVELS for tok in line.split()[:2]):
            hits.append(line)
    return hits


def run_scan(cmd: list[str], env: dict[str, str] | None = None) -> int:
    """Run gitleaks (in `env`, else this environment), relay its output, and return the gate verdict: gitleaks'
    own exit code, except that an exit 0 beside an error-level log line is a failed scan (2), never a clean one."""
    p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace", env=env)
    sys.stdout.write(p.stdout)
    sys.stdout.flush()
    sys.stderr.write(p.stderr)
    sys.stderr.flush()
    errors = error_lines(f"{p.stdout}\n{p.stderr}")
    rc = p.returncode
    # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P0.3.1] an errored scan exits 2 (the "scan did not run as intended" code
    # of a missing binary or config), never 1: a leak finding and a scan that read nothing stay
    # distinguishable in the hook output and in the self-test; a finding beside an error keeps its 1.
    if errors:
        fail("gitleaks logged an error - a scan that errored may have read nothing, so it is never reported "
             "clean: " + " | ".join(errors[:3]))
        if rc == 0:
            return 2
    if rc == 0:
        out("no leaks found")
    elif rc == 1:
        fail("a secret/credential was detected - the push/commit is blocked (rotate the secret; it is NOT enough to amend)")
    else:
        fail(f"gitleaks errored (rc={rc})")
    return rc


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="G2 gitleaks secrets-scan driver (P0.3.1)")
    mode = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=True)
    mode.add_argument("--staged", action="store_true", help="L1 pre-commit: scan the staged diff")
    mode.add_argument("--range", action="store_true", help="L2 pre-push: scan the unpushed range")
    mode.add_argument("--dir", action="store_true", help="L4 CI: scan the whole working tree")
    mode.add_argument("--history", action="store_true", help="L5 nightly: scan the entire commit history")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    gl = gitleaks_bin()
    if gl is None:
        fail("pinned gitleaks binary not found - run scripts/install-gate-tools")
        return 2
    if not CONFIG.is_file():
        fail(f"{CONFIG} missing")
        return 2

    # the scan source gitleaks reads a `.gitleaksignore` beside: the tree for `dir`, the working directory for `git .`
    # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P0.3.1] a refusal (exit 2, the scan-did-not-run code), never a scan beside it: no
    # gitleaks flag stops that read, and deleting a file the developer placed is not this driver's call
    beside = ignore_file_beside(ROOT if args.dir else Path.cwd())
    if beside is not None:
        fail(f"{beside} exists - gitleaks reads a `.gitleaksignore` beside the scan source whatever "
             "--gitleaks-ignore-path says, and a fingerprint listed there drops the finding outside the .gitleaks.toml "
             "allowlist check-gitleaks-allowlist freezes; remove it (an accepted finding goes through that allowlist "
             "or the baseline)")
        return 2

    flags: list[str] = []
    if args.history:
        # a SHALLOW checkout would silently scan only the tip - assert the full history is present
        # at RUNTIME (stronger than a static fetch-depth: 0 YAML lint: it catches the real state).
        rc, shallow = git("rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository")
        if rc == 0 and shallow == "true":
            fail("history scan on a SHALLOW checkout — only the tip would be scanned; the CI job MUST "
                 "check out with fetch-depth: 0")
            return 2
        out("scanning the ENTIRE commit history")
    elif args.staged:
        flags = ["--staged"]
    elif args.range:
        base = resolve_base()
        if base is None:
            out("no range base resolved (first push / empty remote) - FULL-scanning every commit")
        else:
            out(f"scanning the unpushed range {base}..HEAD")
            flags = [f"--log-opts={base}..HEAD"]

    if args.dir:                                       # only the `git` modes spawn git; `dir` reads the files
        return run_scan([gl, "dir", str(ROOT), *common_args()])
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="run-gitleaks-") as scratch:
        env, why = attribute_blind_env(Path(scratch))
        if env is None:
            fail(why)
            return 2
        return run_scan(git_scan(gl, *flags), env)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
